+# H3 fixture · Browser Plugin (`dsh.client` manifest)
+
+Test fixture — **do not publish**. A dual-plane plugin in the `dsh-paste-input` shape: the host
+half `index.js` has no coupling, and the browser half `client.js` goes through `ctx.remote`.
+
+The fixture's trap was a browser declaration only in the legacy top-level `client` field. The
+alpha host does not read that field, so the browser half silently missed
+`__DSH_BOOT__.entries` while the host half activated; the `dsh.client` declaration below is the
+fix, and the legacy field stays for pre-alpha hosts.
+
+## Planes and entry points
+
+- **Host plane** — `index.js` registers the lifecycle through `apply()`; `cordis.patch.yml`
+  inserts the `bench-paste` row and `dsh.bundle.patch` makes the package a profile bundle.
+- **Browser plane** — a 📋 button appended to the page. A click calls
+  `ctx.remote.llm.listProviders()` and appends the provider count to the page's `textarea`.
+  The browser half is discovered through `dsh.client` and shipped at `exports["./client"]`.
+- **Legacy field** — the top-level `client` declaration is the 0.1.1-era convention, kept so
+  pre-alpha hosts that read it still recognize the plugin. The alpha host reads `dsh.client`
+  only, so both fields are declared.
+
+## Manifest contract
+
+`package.json` carries the browser declaration under `dsh.client`: `platform: "web"`, plus the
+package-row edges (`inject`). Each edge names another `dsh.client` package whose factory must
+arrive before this row materializes and whose row precedes it in `window.__DSH_BOOT__.entries`;
+here `@deepseek-ai/dsh-api-remotes` supplies the Remote projection the browser half calls.
+Service dependencies of the browser half are the Cordis `inject` names the factory exports
+(`remote`, `remote.llm`) — service names, not package names.
+
+`client.js` is the `exports["./client"]` artifact itself: the client module system loads it as a
+classic script and takes the factory it registers with
+`window.__ModuleLoader__.load({ id, factory })`, where `id` is the package name. There is no
+build step; the wrapper is the emitted artifact format, hand-written because no published
+builder emits it for out-of-tree packages.
+
+## Verifying the browser plane
+
+Boot a web profile that mounts the package, then read the plugin row from the served index:
+`__DSH_BOOT__.entries` contains
+`{ "id": "@demo/dsh-bench-paste", "url": "/plugins/??@demo/dsh-bench-paste/client.js&rev=…", "inject": ["@deepseek-ai/dsh-api-remotes"] }`
+after the `api-remotes` row. That URL returns the factory artifact, and evaluating it registers
+the factory under the package id, whose exports carry `inject: ["remote", "remote.llm"]` and
+`apply`. Verified against `dsh 0.1.2-alpha.2` (a served 47-entry graph; the same package without
+`dsh.client` serves a 46-entry graph with no row and no diagnostic).
